The equiangular pentagon has interior angles of 108 degrees, and the sum of the squares of its side lengths is given as 308, while the sum of the squares of its diagonal lengths is 800. Although the pentagon is not regular, the given conditions imply that the square of the perimeter is 1540, expressed as \(1540 \sqrt{1}\), where \(m = 1540\), \(n = 1\), and \(n\) is SquareFree.

This results in \(m + n = 1540 + 1 = 1541\).

\boxed{1541}
